\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{quadratic-forms}

\taxon{Definition}
\title{Rational diagonal subgroups}

\p{Let #{Q} be a nondegenerate quadratic form on a finite-dimensional
rational space #{V}, and let #{U} be a
\vocabk{compatible compact-open reference tuple}{fcap-004C}.
For #{G=O,SO,\Spin}, write #{G(Q)} for the rational group,
#{g_p} for the scalar extension of #{g\in G(Q)} to #{\mathbb Q_p},
and #{g_\infty} for its extension to #{\mathbb R}. Spin always means
the actual vector-generated even reverse-norm-one carrier.
The \newvocab{rational diagonal homomorphisms} are

##{\begin{aligned}
\delta_f^G:G(Q)&\longrightarrow G_f(U),
&g&\longmapsto(g_p)_p,\\
\delta_{\mathbb A}^G:G(Q)&\longrightarrow G_{\mathbb A}(U),
&g&\longmapsto(g_\infty,(g_p)_p),\\
\delta_R^G:G(Q)&\longrightarrow G_f^R(U),
&g&\longmapsto(g_p)_{p\notin R},
\end{aligned}}

where #{R} is any set of rational primes. The targets are the finite,
full and away groups of \ref{fcap-004F}, \ref{fcap-004H} and
\ref{fcap-004G}. Their images are the corresponding
\newvocab{rational diagonal subgroups}.}

\p{For O and Spin, the tuple axioms put each rational point in its
reference subgroup at all but finitely many primes. For SO the same
assertion follows from #{U_p^{SO}=i_p^{-1}(U_p^O)}, because
determinant commutes with scalar extension. Thus the finite tuples
are restricted. Deleting coordinates preserves this property, and
the real factor imposes no reference condition. Scalar extension
preserves products and identities, proving the homomorphism assertions.}

\p{For the hyperbolic form #{Q(x,y)=xy}, use the standard-basis references.
The matrix #{g=\operatorname{diag}(2,1/2)} gives a rational SO diagonal.
Its finite components are integral except at #{2}, as computed in
\ref{fcap-004D}. The real component is the same rational matrix
viewed over #{\mathbb R}. Deleting the #{2}-coordinate puts its
away image in the product of the remaining reference subgroups.}

\p{The finite and full diagonals are injective. The away diagonal is
injective whenever at least one prime lies outside #{R}; in particular
this holds for finite #{R}. If every prime is removed, its target is
the singleton group, so the away map is injective only when #{G(Q)}
is itself trivial.}

\subtree{
\taxon{Proof}
\p{Scalar extension to any one retained completion detects a rational
O or SO element: in a rational basis, equality of the extended matrices
implies equality of every rational entry, because the field map is
injective. For Spin, the Clifford scalar-extension isomorphism of
\ref{fcap-003V} identifies its underlying Clifford map with

##{\Cl_{\mathbb Q}(Q)\longrightarrow
L\otimes_{\mathbb Q}\Cl_{\mathbb Q}(Q),
\qquad c\longmapsto1\otimes c,}

where #{L=\mathbb R} or #{\mathbb Q_p}. This map is injective:
the extension of a rational vector-space basis is an #{L}-basis,
so zero extended coefficients force every original coefficient to
vanish. Restricting to the actual Spin carrier preserves injectivity.
Equality of diagonal tuples therefore implies equality at one
completion and hence equality of their rational arguments. The finite
product retains, for example, the prime #{2}; the full product also
retains the real place. The away argument requires the stated
nonempty complement. The empty product is a singleton.}
}

\p{The diagonals commute with the component maps
#{\Spin\to SO\to O} of \ref{fcap-004J}. At each place this is the
scalar-extension action identity of \ref{fcap-003N}; for SO to O it
is the equality of underlying matrices. The full-to-finite projection
and coordinate restriction satisfy

##{\pi_f\delta_{\mathbb A}^G=\delta_f^G,
\qquad r_R\delta_f^G=\delta_R^G.}

These are equalities of homomorphisms, with no topology assigned to
#{G(Q)}. If the rational domain is given the discrete topology, the
maps are continuous; injectivity alone does not make a map a
topological embedding.}

\p{The rational-point diagonal in classical affine full adelic
points is discussed in \citet{Example 2.3, p. 3}{conrad2011adelic}.
Here restricted membership follows directly from the chosen tuple
axioms. TauCeti constructs the finite diagonals in
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.finiteAdelicOrthogonalDiagonal},
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.finiteAdelicSpecialOrthogonalDiagonal}
and \leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.finiteAdelicSpinDiagonal}.
The full orthogonal construction is
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.fullAdelicOrthogonalDiagonal};
the away injectivity condition is explicit in
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.awayAdelicSpecialOrthogonalDiagonal_injective}.
The Spin scalar-extension input is
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.spinGroupBaseChange_injective},
whose faithful, flat scalar-extension hypotheses hold for these fields.
The SO and Spin full and away maps use the same construction.}
